From: PS5_Proton
Subject: [PATCH] server: the title's stop logs off the desktop session

The title asks a game to close at a stop request (the run's budget, a pad
script's stop) with input type 4, WM_CLOSE to the foreground window. In
the Mihawk OS desktop session that is never the session's end: with no
program left the foreground window is one of Explorer's own, which takes
the close and goes on. Explorer's main thread woke at the request and
slept again in its message wait; 10 s later the title ended the session's
seven helpers by SIGKILL, with no clean restart
(two runs of 2026-10-18; record removed from the tree; in git history).

Type 8 logs off as Start's Power and the gate's probes do: WM_CLOSE to the
shell window, Progman, whose close ends Explorer's message loop and
Explorer, the session's root; the title then returns to the launcher by
its normal path. With Explorer unloaded in game mode there is no shell
window, and the game is asked to close as by type 4; the session ends
once the game has gone (src/pw_desktop_session.h).
---
diff --git a/server/queue.c b/server/queue.c
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -3481,7 +3481,8 @@
  * owns it. type 1 key (code: virtual key, scan code << 8, extended << 16), 2 relative mouse motion (x, y), 3 mouse
  * button (code 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle, 3 X1, 4 X2), 4 close: WM_CLOSE to the foreground window, as a user closing
  * it would, 6 the wheels (x horizontal, y vertical, WHEEL_DELTA units), 7 one process by its native pid (code):
- * its windows asked to close (down 1) or the process ended. The cursor it leaves is kept
+ * its windows asked to close (down 1) or the process ended, 8 the desktop session's log off: WM_CLOSE to the shell
+ * window, as Start's Power does, or with no shell to the foreground window as 4. The cursor it leaves is kept
  * for pw_wineserver_cursor. */
 /* The same authoritative input desktop used by hardware injection. */
 struct desktop *ps5_hardware_desktop(void) { return get_hardware_input_desktop(0); }
@@ -3503,6 +3504,7 @@
     };
     union hw_input input;
     struct desktop *desktop;
+    user_handle_t shell;
 
     if (!(desktop = get_hardware_input_desktop( 0 ))) return;
     if (!set_input_desktop( desktop->winstation, desktop ))
@@ -3564,6 +3566,16 @@
             queue_mouse_message( desktop, 0, &input, IMO_HARDWARE, NULL, 0 );
         }
         break;
+    case 8:
+        /* The shell's close ends Explorer, the session's root, and the session with it. WM_CLOSE to the foreground
+         * window does not: with no program left that is one of Explorer's own windows (2026-10-18:
+         * its main thread took it and slept again). With Explorer unloaded in game mode the game is asked, as by 4. */
+        if ((shell = ps5_shell_window( desktop )))
+        {
+            post_message( shell, WM_CLOSE, 0, 0 );
+            break;
+        }
+        /* fall through */
     case 4:
         if (desktop->foreground_input && desktop->foreground_input->shared->active)
             post_message( desktop->foreground_input->shared->active, WM_CLOSE, 0, 0 );
diff --git a/server/user.h b/server/user.h
--- a/server/user.h
+++ b/server/user.h
@@ -173,6 +173,7 @@
 extern struct process *get_top_window_owner( struct desktop *desktop );
 #ifdef __PROSPERO__
 extern unsigned int ps5_close_process_windows( struct desktop *desktop, struct process *process );
+extern user_handle_t ps5_shell_window( struct desktop *desktop );
 #endif
 extern void get_virtual_screen_rect( struct desktop *desktop, struct rectangle *rect, int is_raw );
 extern void post_desktop_message( struct desktop *desktop, unsigned int message,
diff --git a/server/window.c b/server/window.c
--- a/server/window.c
+++ b/server/window.c
@@ -1318,6 +1318,13 @@
     }
     return count;
 }
+
+/* The desktop's shell window (Progman in the Mihawk session), or 0, for the title's log off (queue.c,
+ * ps5_inject_input). */
+user_handle_t ps5_shell_window( struct desktop *desktop )
+{
+    return desktop->shell_window ? desktop->shell_window->handle : 0;
+}
 #endif
 
 /* same as is_visible but takes a window handle */
